---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Info" lang="en" nofooter>
    <article class="post-content">
        <h1>Info</h1>
        {/* Two paragraphs. The first names him and greets the reader; the second says
            what this place is and admits he cannot say what he writes about.

            A blue pen from his father, used to score well on an exam, now in a
            box, and genuinely liked — that was here for a while, and it was
            the only sentence on this page that no one else could have written.
            It was cut because it did not earn its place: it said who he was
            before the page had any reason to care, and it made the admission
            in the last line read as an excuse rather than as a fact. It was
            better as the opening of an essay than as a line of self-description.
            If it comes back it belongs in a post, not here.

            The authorship guarantee moved to /privacy under a Writing heading,
            where it sits beside the other claims about this site that can be
            checked (self-hosted font, static files on Cloudflare Pages) rather
            than here, where it read as a credential.

            Cut from earlier drafts and still reversible: "This is not a technical
            blog" (a warning about what you will not find, not an invitation), and
            the let-go-of image, which was literary in the wrong register for a
            page this plain.

            Still open if wanted later: where he lives, and what he does. Those
            are the only facts left that are true, new, and not derivable from
            the archive.

            One <p> with a break rather than two <p>s: two paragraphs put the
            .875rem bottom margin between them on top of the 1.7 leading, so
            the gap measured 41px — more than double the rhythm of /privacy, and
            it read as two separate blocks on a page that has almost nothing on
            it. The break keeps 27.2px, the site's own line spacing. */}
        <p>
            I'm chenxiaotian. This is where I write.
        </p>

        {/* The authorship guarantee, directly under the introduction and before
            the addresses. It was at the foot of the page first, on the reasoning
            that a colophon goes last, which is where it first went; the order is the
            other way round because the page has three kinds of thing on it and they
            were in the wrong sequence:

              who he is  →  what he wrote  →  how to reach him

            Contact is the last thing a reader needs. Putting the addresses in the
            middle meant the writing claim arrived after them, and the two
            practical blocks sat between the introduction and the one statement
            on this page that is about the work rather than about logistics.
            Telegram in particular reads as a destination — it interrupts.

            It also already appears twice elsewhere: every post ends with a
            write-to-me line carrying that post's title, and the address here is
            the plain fallback. Neither needs to be prominent on this page, so
            it goes last.

            No label on this paragraph. It had "writing:" above it, matching
            "contact:" below, and that was one label too many: a label is worth
            having when the content under it does not already announce itself,
            and this paragraph opens "Every word here was written by me." —
            "writing:" was restating the sentence underneath it. The addresses
            are the opposite case, three tokens that mean nothing without a
            label, which is where the one label on this page now sits.

            The two paragraphs above the addresses are both addressed to the
            reader, so they run together without a break; the label marks the
            start of the part that is for use rather than for reading. That is
            the division the page actually has, and one label is enough to make
            it.

            "Nothing on this site was generated, drafted, or edited by an AI
            system" — and nothing after it. The clause "including these sentences"
            that used to close it is gone, and it was the load-bearing part: it
            extended the claim from the writing to this page's own English, which
            is text an AI drafted from Chinese the author dictated. That made the
            sentence false in the strict reading, which is the only reading a
            claim like this survives or fails on. "Translated" went with it for
            the same reason — turning the dictation into English is the clearest
            case of the thing the sentence says did not happen.

            What remains is exactly what is true: three essays in Chinese, written
            by the author, not machine-generated. The English chrome around them
            is not covered by the claim, and does not need to be. */}
        {/* No .claim rule anywhere: the class names a paragraph that was styled
            once and then not, so it has been carrying no styles for the whole
            life of the page. Kept as a hook for now — the paragraph is the one
            piece of prose on this site that states a fact about itself, and it
            is the first thing that should look different if anything here
            changes. */}
        <p class="claim">
            Every word here was written by me. Nothing on this site was
            generated, drafted, or edited by an AI system.
        </p>
        {/* "contact:" was an <h2>. It was promoted to a real heading so it would
            match "Fonts" and "Hosting" on /privacy, and that was the wrong
            comparison: on /privacy those are sections, because Privacy has three
            of them and each carries a paragraph. About has one section — the
            introduction above — and this is not a second one. It is a label on a
            block of addresses, the same kind of thing as the copyright line in
            the footer, and the h2 said otherwise: a 17.6px 600-weight heading
            over two lines of 16px body, on a page whose only other bold text is
            the h1.

            It is a <p> now, at the same weight as the addresses below it and in
            the tertiary grey, so the three labels read as one column of labels
            — contact:, mail:, telegram: — rather than as a heading followed by
            content. The colon was the tell that it was never a heading. */}
        <p class="contact-label">connect:</p>
        {/* One sentence, both addresses in it. This was two labelled rows —
            "mail:" and "telegram:" each with its own value — and the rows
            carried a lot of apparatus for what they held: a grid wrapper to
            align them, a small-caps @ so one row's leading circle would not
            knock its visual start out of line with the other, and comments
            defending decisions that only mattered because the rows existed.

            In a sentence none of that is needed. Both addresses are the same
            kind of thing, so they take the same kind of treatment: the link
            carries its own hover wash and its own focus ring, and the text
            around them is prose. Splitting an address into "where to go" and
            "who it is for" was tried and dropped for the same reason — both
            halves have to be read to use it. */}
        <p class="reach">
            Reach me at <a href="https://t.me/spriginhand" rel="me noopener" target="_blank">@spriginhand</a> or <a href="mailto:a@worth.ee">a@worth.ee</a>.
        </p>
    </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    /* The block label, one step above the two rows it names. It carries the same
       16px regular weight and the same prose colour as "mail:" and "telegram:"
       below it, so the three read as one column of labels rather than as a
       heading with content under it. The spacing is kept from the h2 it
       replaces — 24px of air above, 8px below — because that rhythm was right
       even when the type was not.

       --block is gone. It set 48px on the first label to break the page after
       the introduction, on the theory that "sentences to the reader" and "facts
       about the site" are two kinds of thing and the spacing should say so. It
       was the only gap on the site above 24px — /privacy runs 20 / 24 / 8 / 24 /
       8 and never opens up — so the one page with two kinds of content on it
       became the page with the different rhythm, which is a worse thing to be
       than the one with the flatter structure. Two flat pages beat one of each. */
    .contact-label {
        margin: 1.5rem 0 .5rem;
    }
    /* The introduction is two paragraphs that set as one block. Their margins
       are both zeroed, which leaves the gap between them at 12px of blank — the
       same as the gap between two lines inside either of them.

       Measured off the screenshot, not the DOM. A 27.2px line box holds about
       15px of glyph and the rest is leading, so every box gap gets 12px of
       blank added to it: 0 → 12px, 14px → 26px, 27.2px → 39px. That is why
       getBoundingClientRect kept reporting a tidy 27px while the page plainly
       had a second blank line in it. The two numbers to compare are always the
       blank-pixel counts, never the box gaps. */
    .post-content > p { margin-bottom: 0; }
    /* The reach line: prose with two links in it.

       These links are underlined, which reverses the decision the address rows
       made. That decision was right THERE and is wrong here: the rows had a
       label on the left and a value on the right, and that structure was itself
       the cue — the wash only had to confirm it on hover. Prose has no such
       structure. Two addresses sitting mid-sentence look like words in it, and
       a wash that only appears on hover is not a resting-state signal at all.

       So the underline is permanent and the wash stays as the hover. Both are
       needed: one says "this is a link", the other says "this one, right now".
       The underline is `text-decoration` rather than a border so it sits at the
       font's own underline position with the offset below it, and it moves with
       the text rather than sitting under the padding box. */
    .reach a {
        color: var(--text-primary);
        text-decoration: underline;
        text-underline-offset: 2px;
        text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
        border-radius: 3px;
        padding: 2px 4px;
        margin: 0 -2px;
        transition: background-color .15s ease;
    }
    /* The underline fades out as the wash comes in, so hovering swaps one cue
       for the other instead of stacking both on top of each other. */
    .reach a:hover {
        background-color: oklch(96% 0 0);
        text-decoration-color: transparent;
    }
    /* The ring goes on the link itself: it is the only focusable thing in the
       sentence, so this is where the keyboard user's attention lands too. */
    .reach a:focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid var(--grey7);
        outline-offset: 1px;
    }
</style>
